WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.030
- What we're doing today is
we're building a dream house

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:07.660
and that really is a translation

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:10.040
of what is important to people.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.510
- It starts with this, or
otherwise it's just some kind

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.703
of abstract kind of
spatial artistic piece,

00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:18.030
it kind of starts with you got people

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.530
who need to be housed or
go to work or do something.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.923
- So, I tend to like really simple forms,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.660
and I'm intentionally choosing
the same color as the base

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:32.150
just so it kind of looks like
there's a seamless connection

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.180
between the base and this mass.

00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.220
- The house is really kind of
most simple type of building

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.220
one could build, you know
a space for your bed,

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:42.150
a space for a kitchen,
a space for a bathroom,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:45.770
so because it's so simple it
actually can be a container

00:00:45.770 --> 00:00:47.350
for really, really big ideas.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.920
- So the first thing that
you want to think about

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.963
your ambition, your vision
of what your idea is,

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.990
so what I'm trying to do, and
we'll see how successfully

00:00:55.990 --> 00:00:59.240
we do this, is build a
house around a library.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.930
- I draw inspiration from traveling,

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.750
so when I walk around I pay attention

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.400
to environments and spaces.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:05.660
I don't really notice people.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.750
- If you look at this, these
are a flow of material pieces

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:09.980
that can be built up into something,

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:11.590
and taken apart and used again,

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.080
and the future of architecture

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.560
really should be more like this.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.930
Something that you kind of engage with

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:18.390
rather than something being hidden.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:22.960
- So I'm sticking with a very
monochromatic color palette

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.803
which is kind of very
typical of architects.

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.500
Like these really bright
colors scare me a little bit.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:31.723
- The yellow is a color that
will stand out the most,

00:01:31.723 --> 00:01:33.680
so we're going with yellow

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:35.650
as our most important element today.

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.880
- It's not just the visual things,

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.210
I mean you're looking at pattern
and color and all of that,

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:41.510
but it's the way that it
feels when you kind of walk in

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:43.540
like the feeling of a
wood floor underneath you

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.550
is radically different
from a piece of stone.

00:01:45.550 --> 00:01:48.830
I mean we do have whole
regimes of sensory input

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.590
that we're subject to
everyday that we don't pay

00:01:50.590 --> 00:01:52.133
that much attention to, but if you do

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:54.350
it kind of changes the
way that you see things.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.500
- Design is definitely
an iterative process,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:01:59.120
you know you kind of
have to test your ideas.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.293
Your first one is usually
not gonna be great

00:02:01.293 --> 00:02:03.433
(laughs) which is fine.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.090
- Being able to iterate
is absolutely crucial

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:08.630
to what it is we do because you possibly

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.470
cannot get it right the first time.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.720
- One of the things about
architecture that's kind of fun

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.770
is when things don't go right.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.580
It has to be the thing that
kind of turns your gears

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.590
when you're faced with
a limit or something

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.497
you kind of stop and say,

00:02:20.497 --> 00:02:23.053
"Hmm, really, does that have to be so?

00:02:23.097 --> 00:02:25.100
"Does that necessarily
have to be a limit?"

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.170
- Creativity can tend to come
out of a lot of restrictions

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:30.630
just because you have to work within that

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.990
and that's common for
a lot of architecture

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:37.010
cuz you're trying to conform
to a lot of zoning regulations

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.845
and building code, also
client requirements, budget,

00:02:39.845 --> 00:02:41.730
like there's so many constraints

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:44.830
you tend to have to be very
creative with your solutions.

00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:47.427
- It's really a profession
all about where you dream

00:02:47.427 --> 00:02:50.750
and then you see your dream and
your vision come to reality,

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:54.513
to fruition and that's
really quite marvelous.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.600
- Lately a lot of people who've
gotten architecture degrees

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.550
have left the profession and have gone

00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:00.653
into game design and things like that.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.140
Some of Nike's head shoe designers

00:03:03.140 --> 00:03:04.750
were trained as architects.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.150
- And it's really important for us

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:08.070
to understand architecture the way

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.630
we would understand music, the
way we would understand dance

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:13.720
because it is the beautiful
qualities of the spaces

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:15.460
we live in, we work in, we play in,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.210
it's constantly surrounding us.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.459
- Like Instagram and social
media, there's a lot of feature

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:21.530
on architecture and design now,

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.170
so people are starting to
see it as something of value

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.193
and why it's worth investing in.

00:03:26.230 --> 00:03:28.020
Most architects that I
know, you kind of see it

00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.863
as a calling, as something
you're very passionate about.

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.770
- So if you imagine someone's
kind of walking across

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.710
the front of there, and I've kind of used

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:37.470
the colors instrumentally but you can see

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.020
the kind of layers of the
house as you move through

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.550
and how the windows are
getting a little bit

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:43.150
smaller and smaller and smaller,

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.530
so as a pedestrian walking by the front

00:03:45.530 --> 00:03:47.700
the house doesn't feel
like it's totally shut off

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.440
that if there's trees and
stuff back here you'd be able

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.480
to see all the way through,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:53.623
and also it creates this
false sense of depth.

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.960
So imagine that this is
the front door, right,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.780
so we have our rather large windows.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:58.960
This could be the kitchen.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.440
Imagine that we've kind
of completed these parts

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:04.610
but that this is kinda still open,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.800
so you have a garden
that's kinda separating

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.950
the public pieces at
the front of the house

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.370
from the more kind of
private pieces of the house.

00:04:11.370 --> 00:04:13.170
That element is actually
also kind of worked

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.530
it's way around out here

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.760
so with here you can
kind of see the depth,

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.700
so again we're trying to
create these connections

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:21.040
through this way and kind
of stitch the building

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.733
together from a conceptual
experience point of view,

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.150
so there is your mock-up for a house.

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:28.550
- This is my house (laughs).

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:33.500
So this is like the living
area in this white block,

00:04:33.500 --> 00:04:35.570
and this is the main mass

00:04:35.570 --> 00:04:37.980
that sort of providing
shading for the building.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:42.453
I did leave a lot of openings
on this top portion to allow,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.700
I'm imagining sky lights and things here,

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:47.350
so there's lots of like
deep light penetration,

00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:50.220
so this is sort of like
a little car park area,

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.530
and then this is just more
to frame an open courtyard

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:55.580
that would be landscape and outdoor area.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.980
- So all this yellow box is my library

00:04:57.980 --> 00:04:59.960
that extends through the entire side,

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.210
and it holds and brings
the whole house together

00:05:04.210 --> 00:05:06.240
and then the house is
not necessarily divided

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:08.330
into living rooms, or
dining rooms or bedrooms.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:09.580
Most of the house feels like it's blank.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:10.950
What I'm imagining there that there's

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:14.150
a big piece of glass in here
so that glass allows you

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.380
to look at the water,
hear the water singing,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.573
have the sea figuratively
come into the house.

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.570
Back in here is what I'm
imagining is where you enter from,

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:26.483
so this is space where
we share our library.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:30.040
That's a space where I
experience it only by myself.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:32.130
No one really else is
allowed to go in there,

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:35.273
so the library really does
extend through the whole piece,

00:05:35.273 --> 00:05:36.890
and it's really something

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:38.883
that brings the experience together.

00:05:41.712 --> 00:05:44.212
(light music)

